arkinsonism; Increased neck tone and axial rigidity (++); Early dysphagia; Early cognitive features including apathy, decreased verbal fluency, and pseudobulbar effect; Early frontal release signs on neurologic examination; \"Hummingbird sign\" on sagittal brain MRI (++)":["u355"],"c61-compare-psp|row:PSP|TD|Alien limb syndrome; Hallucinations; Early cerebellar signs; Early dysautonomia":["u356"],"c61-compare-cbs|row:CBS|TH|CBS":["u124"],"c61-compare-cbs|row:CBS|TD|Asymmetric motor findings at presentation (+); Limb dystonia at presentation (+); Limb myoclonus (++); Alien-limb phenomenon (+++)":["u357"],"c61-compare-cbs|row:CBS|TD|Cortical sensory deficit (++); Frontal lobe cognitive symptoms including executive dysfunction, behavioral/personality changes, aphasia (++); Focal cortical parietofrontal atrophy on brain MRI":["u358"],"c61-compare-cbs|row:CBS|TD|Rest tremor; Good response to dopaminergic medications; Hallucinations; Cerebellar signs; Prominent dysautonomia":["u359"],"c61-compare-msa|row:MSA|TH|MSA":["u128"],"c61-compare-msa|row:MSA|TD|Cerebellar features on examination (++); Prominent dysautonomia (+++); Hyperreflexia and other pyramidal tract findings (+)":["u360"],"c61-compare-msa|row:MSA|TD|Relatively few cognitive features; Respiratory stridor (++); Orofacial dyskinesias; \"Hot cross bun\" sign on axial brainstem MRI":["u361"],"c61-compare-msa|row:MSA|TD|Dysautonomia only in relation to medications; Hallucinations":["u362"],"c61-compare-dlb|row:DLB|TH|DLB":["u132"],"c61-compare-dlb|row:DLB|TD|Hallucinations (+++); Fluctuations in level of alertness (++); Dementia within 1 y of the onset of motor features of parkinsonism":["u363"],"c61-compare-dlb|row:DLB|TD|REM sleep behavior disorder (+); Sensitivity to medications, especially neuroleptics (++)":["u364"],"c61-compare-dlb|row:DLB|TD|Active delirium explained by concurrent medications or an alternative process; Supranuclear gaze palsy":["u365"],"pd-pathology|Pathology and atypical syndromes":["u4"],"pd-cognition|Cognition, psychosis and the one-year rule":["u5"],"pd-motor-treatment|Motor treatment in older adults":["u6"],"c61-levodopa-agonist-contrast|row:Decision|TH|Decision":["u145"],"c61-levodopa-agonist-contrast|row:Decision|TH|Levodopa":["u146"],"c61-levodopa-agonist-contrast|row:Decision|TH|Dopamine agonists":["u147"],"c61-levodopa-agonist-contrast|row:Hallucinations, confusion and psychosis|TH|Hallucinations, confusion and psychosis":["u148"],"c61-levodopa-agonist-contrast|row:Hallucinations, confusion and psychosis|TD|Lower risk than agonists; the chapter favours levodopa as the safer choice in patients older than 70 years.":["u149"],"c61-levodopa-agonist-contrast|row:Hallucinations, confusion and psychosis|TD|Higher risk, particularly in older adults.":["u150"],"c61-levodopa-agonist-contrast|row:Dyskinesias and wearing-off|TH|Dyskinesias and wearing-off":["u151"],"c61-levodopa-agonist-contrast|row:Dyskinesias and wearing-off|TD|More likely than with agonists.":["u152"],"c61-levodopa-agonist-contrast|row:Dyskinesias and wearing-off|TD|Less likely, attributed to lower potency and a longer half-life.":["u153"],"c61-dopaminergic-agents|row:Class|TH|Class":["u154"],"c61-dopaminergic-agents|row:Class|TH|Agents":["u155"],"c61-dopaminergic-agents|row:Dopamine precursor|TH|Dopamine precursor":["u156"],"c61-dopaminergic-agents|row:Dopamine precursor|TD|Levodopa":["u157"],"c61-dopaminergic-agents|row:Peripheral decarboxylase inhibitors|TH|Peripheral decarboxylase inhibitors":["u158"],"c61-dopaminergic-agents|row:Peripheral decarboxylase inhibitors|TD|Carbidopa; benserazide":["u159"],"c61-dopaminergic-agents|row:Dopamine agonists|TH|Dopamine agonists":["u160"],"c61-dopaminergic-agents|row:Dopamine agonists|TD|Pramipexole; ropinirole; rotigotine; apomorphine":["u161"],"c61-dopaminergic-agents|row:COMT inhibitors|TH|COMT inhibitors":["u162"],"c61-dopaminergic-agents|row:COMT inhibitors|TD|Entacapone; opicapone":["u163"],"c61-dopaminergic-agents|row:Dopamine releaser|TH|Dopamine releaser":["u164"],"c61-dopaminergic-agents|row:Dopamine releaser|TD|Amantadine":["u165"],"c61-dopaminergic-agents|row:Peripheral dopamine receptor blocker|TH|Peripheral dopamine receptor blocker":["u166"],"c61-dopaminergic-agents|row:Peripheral dopamine receptor blocker|TD|Domperidone":["u167"],"c61-dopaminergic-agents|row:MAO type B inhibitors|TH|MAO type B inhibitors":["u168"],"c61-dopaminergic-agents|row:MAO type B inhibitors|TD|Selegiline; selegiline orally disintegrating; rasagiline; safinamide":["u169"],"c61-dopaminergic-agents|row:A2A antagonist|TH|A2A antagonist":["u170"],"c61-dopaminergic-agents|row:A2A antagonist|TD|Istradefylline":["u171"],"pd-adjuncts|Adjuncts and agonists: what each adds":["u172"],"pd-amantadine|Amantadine: separate the treatment goals":["u173"],"c61-amantadine-regimen|row:Purpose|TH|Purpose":["u174"],"c61-amantadine-regimen|row:Purpose|TH|Chapter regimen":["u175"],"c61-amantadine-regimen|row:Anti-Parkinson symptomatic treatment|TH|Anti-Parkinson symptomatic treatment":["u176"],"c61-amantadine-regimen|row:Anti-Parkinson symptomatic treatment|TD|Usually 100 mg twice daily.":["u177"],"c61-amantadine-regimen|row:Antidyskinetic treatment|TH|Antidyskinetic treatment":["u178"],"c61-amantadine-regimen|row:Antidyskinetic treatment|TD|The chapter describes 300–400 mg/day; in its peak-dose dyskinesia discussion, start at 100 mg twice daily and increase up to 200 mg twice daily. Apply the renal and older-adult precautions above.":["u179"],"pd-fluctuations|Motor and nonmotor offs; dyskinesia through the dose cycle":["u252"],"dyskinesia-forms|Chorea and dystonia: describe the movement before its timing":["u253"],"copy|row:Type|TH|Type":["u254"],"copy|row:Type|TH|Timing and appearance":["u255"],"copy|row:Type|TH|Treatment distinction":["u256"],"c61-timing-peak|row:Peak-dose dyskinesia|TH|Peak-dose dyskinesia":["u257"],"c61-timing-peak|row:Peak-dose dyskinesia|TD|At peak plasma concentrations of levodopa or a dopamine agonist, when synaptic brain dopamine is excessive.":["u258"],"c61-timing-peak|row:Peak-dose dyskinesia|TD|Reduce the individual dose; smaller doses may need to be given more frequently.":["u259"],"c61-timing-diphasic|row:Diphasic dyskinesia|TH|Diphasic dyskinesia":["u260"],"c61-timing-diphasic|row:Diphasic dyskinesia|TD|At the beginning and end of the dose, not at peak plasma and brain levels. The legs are particularly affected, often by a mixture of chorea and dystonia.":["u261"],"c61-timing-diphasic|row:Diphasic dyskinesia|TD|Recognise the beginning/end-of-dose pattern before treating it as peak-dose excess.":["u262"],"c61-timing-off|row:Off dyskinesia / off dystonia|TH|Off dyskinesia / off dystonia":["u263"],"c61-timing-off|row:Off dyskinesia / off dystonia|TD|Occurs during an off period; early-morning off dystonia is an important treatment exception.":["u264"],"c61-timing-off|row:Off dyskinesia / off dystonia|TD|Early-morning off dystonia responds well to dopaminergic therapy. For other off dyskinesias, the chapter encourages consideration of DBS in suitable candidates.":["u265"],"dopaminergic-nonmotor|Dopaminergic medication: nonmotor complications":["u266"],"pd-psychosis|Psychosis and sleep":["u7"],"pd-depression|Depression in Parkinson disease":["u244"],"pd-procedures|Surgical therapy: selection, stimulation and ablation":["u267"],"focused-ultrasound|Focused ultrasound: retain the date, correct the target and mechanism":["u268"],"copy|row:Procedure|TH|Procedure":["u269"],"copy|row:Procedure|TH|Reversibility and titration":["u270"],"copy|row:Procedure|TH|Long-term trade-off":["u271"],"c61-compare-dbs|row:DBS|TH|DBS":["u272"],"c61-compare-dbs|row:DBS|TD|Stimulation can be reprogrammed or switched off. This reversibility concerns stimulation; it does not undo implantation or all surgical complications.":["u273"],"c61-compare-dbs|row:DBS|TD|Requires implanted hardware and ongoing programming; generator replacement may be needed. Settings can be adjusted as symptoms change.":["u274"],"c61-compare-fus|row:Focused ultrasound|TH|Focused ultrasound":["u275"],"c61-compare-fus|row:Focused ultrasound|TD|Creates a permanent, nonprogrammable lesion; it cannot be switched off or titrated afterward like DBS.":["u276"],"c61-compare-fus|row:Focused ultrasound|TD|No IPG replacement or stimulation-programming visits, but less ability to adapt the treatment to a changing pattern of disabling symptoms.":["u277"],"copy|CHAPTER 61 / HAZZARD":["u9"],"c61-opening|Start with the motor syndrome, then look for the pattern and the exceptions.":["u10"],"c61-opening|Early cognitive, autonomic, gaze or postural problems can redirect the diagnosis; a treatment response is useful evidence but is not the whole diagnosis.":["u11"],"c61-motor-features|Parkinsonism can be defined as a variable combination of six specific, independent motor features: bradykinesia, tremor at rest, rigidity, loss of postural reflexes, flexed posture, and freezing of gait.":["u12"],"c61-motor-criteria|TABLE 61-4 lists bradykinesia, rigidity and rest tremor without strength marks; asymmetric onset alone carries (++).":["u366"],"c61-motor-criteria|Its footnote says that not all inclusion criteria are required for a diagnosis.":["u367"],"c61-older-prevalence|Idiopathic Parkinson disease is estimated to affect approximately 1% to 2% of people older than age 60.":["u15"],"c61-diagnostic-error|Longitudinal clinical postmortem studies suggest that 10% to 20% of patients thought to have Parkinson disease in life will have alternative diagnoses on autopsy.":["u16"],"c61-incidental-lewy-bodies|Midbrain Lewy bodies are seen on autopsy in about 20% of older individuals without a known history of parkinsonism.":["u17"],"c61-sex-ratio|Parkinson disease is about 1.5 times more common in men than in women.":["u18"],"c61-sporadic|Over 90% of cases of Parkinson disease are considered sporadic.":["u19"],"c61-inverse-risk-associations|Factors consistently associated with lower PD risk in the chapter are cigarette smoking, caffeinated coffee consumption, high plasma uric acid levels and endogenous estrogen exposure.":["u328"],"c61-inverse-risk-associations|The biological principles mediating these associations are incompletely understood.":["u329"],"c61-inverse-risk-associations|These are associations, not recommendations to smoke, raise urate or prescribe estrogen for prevention.":["u330"],"c61-park-gene-snapshot|At the time of the chapter’s account, 20 PARK genes were implicated in PD risk.":["u331"],"c61-park-gene-snapshot|This is the book’s historical count, not a current claim about the size of the gene catalogue.":["u332"],"c61-gba-allelic-variation|The chapter links GBA allelic variations, including deletions as well as point mutations, to early-onset PD, particularly among people of Ashkenazi Jewish ancestry.":["u333"],"c61-pd-pathogenic-mechanisms|Mechanisms discussed include autophagy–lysosomal dysfunction, oxidative stress, network frailty, prion-like spread of toxic alpha-synuclein pathology, dopamine toxicity and selective neuronal vulnerability.":["u334"],"c61-pd-pathogenic-mechanisms|These are proposed pathogenic mechanisms, not a claim that one mechanism explains every case.":["u335"],"c61-heterogeneous-parkinsonism|Conditions with heterogeneous manifestations that can include parkinsonism include Wilson disease, juvenile Huntington disease and spinocerebellar ataxia type 3.":["u336"],"c61-heterogeneous-parkinsonism|This is a set of chapter examples, not an exhaustive differential.":["u337"],"c61-symmetry|A symmetrical onset of symptoms is a helpful clue that one is likely dealing with an alternative cause of parkinsonism other than idiopathic Parkinson disease.":["u20"],"c61-corticobasal-asymmetry|Corticobasal syndrome is one of the exceptions to the general rule that asymmetric limb motor involvement favors a diagnosis of Parkinson disease.":["u21"],"c61-tremor|The tremor caused by Parkinson disease is a predominant rest tremor, whereas essential tremor is not typically present at rest.":["u22"],"c61-dat-imaging|Dopamine transporter single-photon emission computed tomography imaging has been approved to distinguish essential tremor from Parkinson disease in patients with atypical tremor manifestation.":["u23"],"c61-scan-limits|An abnormal dopamine-transporter scan supports a presynaptic dopaminergic deficit; it does not by itself distinguish idiopathic Parkinson disease from every atypical degenerative parkinsonian disorder.":["u24"],"c61-comparison-footnote|This table summarises published diagnostic criteria.":["u136"],"c61-comparison-footnote|Inclusion and exclusion lists are not exhaustive; not all inclusion features are required for a diagnosis, and a single exclusion feature is not an absolute contraindication to that diagnosis.":["u137"],"c61-comparison-footnote|Plus signs indicate the strength of association between a feature and a disorder.":["u138"],"c61-comparison-strength-footnote|+/- denotes strength of association between a particular feature and a specific disorder.":["u368"],"c61-comparison-scope-footnote|The above table presents a summary of published diagnostic criteria.":["u369"],"c61-comparison-scope-footnote|The lists of inclusion and exclusion criteria for any given disorders are not exclusive/exhaustive of all possibilities.":["u370"],"c61-comparison-scope-footnote|For any given disease, not all inclusion criteria are required to make an affirmative diagnosis.":["u371"],"c61-comparison-scope-footnote|Similarly, the presence of a single exclusion criterion does not represent an absolute contraindication to diagnosing a specific disorder.":["u372"],"c61-dopaminergic-loss|By the time motor symptoms of Parkinson disease are present, the posterior putamen has undergone profound denervation equating to the loss of over 60% to 80% of dopaminergic terminals.":["u26"],"c61-braak-stage|Lewy body deposition in the original Braak stage 3 involves the nigra and is thought to correspond with the onset of motor features of Parkinson disease.":["u27"],"c61-tau-disorders|Unlike Parkinson disease, both progressive supranuclear palsy and corticobasal syndrome are not thought to be disorders of α-synuclein but instead are attributable to misfolded tau.":["u28"],"c61-msa-glial-pathology|Multiple system atrophy is characterized by the development of argyrophilic cytoplasmic inclusion bodies that are positive for α-synuclein in glial cells rather than typical neuronal Lewy bodies.":["u29"],"c61-neuronal-loss-scope|SOURCE SCOPE PENDING — the supplied neuronal-loss list names the basal ganglia, substantia nigra, locus coeruleus, pontine nuclei, cerebellar Purkinje cells and intermediolateral cell column of the spinal cord.":["u338"],"c61-neuronal-loss-scope|Its disease context must be confirmed from the chapter paragraph before this is taught as a Parkinson-disease-specific list; the pontocerebellar and spinal-autonomic pattern raises an MSA attribution question.":["u339"],"c61-msa-course|The median age of onset for multiple system atrophy is in the sixth decade of life with a mean time to severe disability of approximately 5 years from the time of diagnosis.":["u30"],"c61-msa-cognition|Unlike other atypical parkinsonian conditions, cognitive impairment is not a typical feature of multiple system atrophy.":["u31"],"c61-early-cognitive-multitasking|Difficulty with multitasking is among the early cognitive changes described in PD; it should be considered alongside the other cognitive domains.":["u340"],"c61-additional-nonmotor-features|Nonmotor manifestations include olfactory impairment, erectile dysfunction and rhinorrhoea.":["u341"],"c61-additional-nonmotor-features|These are further examples, not a complete nonmotor symptom list.":["u342"],"c61-mobility-related-mortality|With restricted mobility, secondary acute comorbidities contributing to death include aspiration pneumonia, traumatic falls, deep vein thrombosis and pulmonary embolus.":["u343"],"c61-mobility-related-mortality|These examples should not be reduced to aspiration alone.":["u344"],"c61-dementia-timing|Chapter 61, TABLE 61-4 (printed p. 941), lists the DLB inclusion criterion as “Dementia within 1 y of the onset of motor features of parkinsonism”.":["u373"],"c61-timing-chapter63|Chapter 63 (printed p. 984) states that research criteria for DLB “require cognitive symptoms that predate parkinsonism by 12 months”.":["u374"],"c61-timing-chapter63|It describes cognitive deterioration in PDD as occurring in established Parkinson disease, typically years to decades after motor features.":["u375"],"c61-timing-clarification|The book is not consistent here: it gives the 1-year rule in opposite directions in these two chapters.":["u376"],"c61-timing-clarification|Chapter 61’s table places dementia within 1 year of motor onset; chapter 63 requires cognitive symptoms to predate parkinsonism by 12 months.":["u377"],"c61-timing-clarification|Both statements are retained with their own source; they are not reconciled and neither is selected as the operative rule.":["u378"],"c61-dlb-core-features|Individuals meet the threshold for probable dementia with Lewy bodies if they show findings of a progressive dementia and have at least two of four core features.":["u35"],"c61-dlb-criteria-scope|This chapter uses a four-core-feature summary.":["u65"],"c61-dlb-criteria-scope|The printed Table 63-3 uses a different core/suggestive-feature framework, now shown in the other neurodegenerative disorders chapter.":["u66"],"c61-dlb-criteria-scope|Do not combine the two frameworks into a new rule.":["u67"],"c61-dementia-ten-years|About 50% of individuals with Parkinson disease will develop dementia within 10 years of their initial diagnosis.":["u38"],"c61-levodopa-distal-dexterity|Fine motor distal dexterity is among the features that improve most with levodopa.":["u345"],"c61-medication-refractory-axial|Axial features that may be medication-refractory include hypophonia, dysphagia and postural instability.":["u346"],"c61-medication-refractory-axial|The response of these features should be assessed separately from improvement in distal dexterity.":["u347"],"c61-treatment-overview|Levodopa and dopamine agonists offer different benefits and harms; the patient’s age and vulnerability to neuropsychiatric adverse effects change the balance.":["u181"],"c61-levodopa-combinations|Sinemet combines carbidopa with levodopa; Madopar combines benserazide with levodopa.":["u182"],"c61-levodopa-combinations|The orally dissolving carbidopa/levodopa preparation enters the stomach in swallowed saliva; it can help with swallowing difficulty.":["u183"],"c61-levodopa-release|Immediate-release levodopa produces a faster, more predictable “on”.":["u184"],"c61-levodopa-release|Extended release has a slightly longer plasma half-life but slower, less predictable onset.":["u185"],"c61-levodopa-release|The two formulations can be combined.":["u186"],"c61-levodopa-complications|Over half of patients develop troublesome complications of disabling response fluctuations or dyskinesias after 5 years of levodopa therapy.":["u39"],"c61-agonist-adverse-effects|Dopamine agonists are more likely than levodopa to cause hallucinations, confusion, and psychosis, especially in older adults.":["u40"],"c61-older-treatment-choice|It is safer to utilize levodopa than dopamine agonists in patients older than 70 years.":["u41"],"c61-antimuscarinic-caution|Antimuscarinic drugs should be avoided in patients older than 70 years due to the risk of cognitive impairment and hallucinations.":["u42"],"c61-antimuscarinic-freezing|Antimuscarinic exposure has also been linked to a higher risk of freezing of gait in PD—a motor reason for caution alongside the class’s other adverse effects.":["u245"],"c61-motor-review|Relate symptoms to the dose cycle: wearing-off, peak-dose dyskinesia and unpredictable off periods are different problems.":["u43"],"c61-motor-review|Check adherence, swallowing and adverse effects before escalating treatment.":["u44"],"c61-entacapone-action|Entacapone prolongs levodopa’s plasma half-life and increases its peak concentration, extending each dose’s effect: more “on”, less “off”.":["u187"],"c61-entacapone-action|The same enhancement may increase dyskinesias, requiring a lower levodopa dose.":["u188"],"c61-entacapone-dose|Entacapone is short acting: the chapter specifies 200 mg taken with each levodopa dose.":["u189"],"c61-entacapone-dose|Stalevo combines entacapone, carbidopa and levodopa.":["u190"],"c61-tolcapone-chapter|The chapter describes tolcapone in 100- and 200-mg tablets, with greater potency and longer action than entacapone, but greater diarrhoea and hepatotoxicity risk.":["u191"],"c61-tolcapone-correction|US-label correction: tolcapone has US prescribing information with a boxed warning for potentially fatal liver failure.":["u192"],"c61-tolcapone-correction|It is ordinarily reserved for patients with levodopa/carbidopa fluctuations who have not responded satisfactorily to, or are unsuitable for, other adjuncts, with liver assessment and monitoring.":["u193"],"c61-tolcapone-correction|Do not learn the supplied “removed from the US market” statement as current fact.":["u194"],"c61-ergot-agonists|Ergot agonists—pergolide, bromocriptine and cabergoline—can cause cardiac valvulopathy and retroperitoneal, pleuropulmonary or pericardial fibrosis.":["u195"],"c61-ergot-agonists|The chapter does not recommend them for PD and records pergolide’s US withdrawal.":["u196"],"c61-pramipexole-ropinirole|Pramipexole and ropinirole have similar efficacy at therapeutic levels, with slow-release preparations available.":["u197"],"c61-pramipexole-ropinirole|Compared with levodopa, agonists more often cause daytime drowsiness, sudden sleep attacks—including while driving—and ankle oedema.":["u198"],"c61-agonist-impulse-control|Ask specifically about agonist-associated impulse-control problems: hypersexuality, gambling, compulsive shopping and binge eating.":["u199"],"c61-rotigotine-delivery|Rotigotine is delivered by a patch on the upper torso or arms.":["u200"],"c61-rotigotine-delivery|Steadier exposure can help swallowing difficulty, motor fluctuations and nocturnal akinesia when the last bedtime levodopa dose does not last through the night.":["u201"],"c61-rotigotine-receptors|Rotigotine has high-potency D1, D2 and D3 agonism and lower D4 and D5 activity.":["u202"],"c61-rotigotine-receptors|Unlike pramipexole and ropinirole, it has D1 and D5 activity, resembling apomorphine.":["u203"],"c61-rotigotine-receptors|The chapter proposes D1 activity as a possible explanation for benefit in freezing of gait; this is a mechanistic explanation, not proof of superiority.":["u204"],"c61-apomorphine-rescue|Apomorphine is a powerful agonist used for rapid relief of disabling “off” states; intense nausea is a major limitation.":["u205"],"c61-apomorphine-rescue|The chapter describes subcutaneous rescue and the sublingual film Kynmobi.":["u206"],"c61-apomorphine-rescue|The film’s mention records the formulation described in the book, not current market availability.":["u207"],"c61-domperidone-role|Domperidone acts peripherally at dopamine receptors to reduce gastrointestinal upset from levodopa and agonists.":["u208"],"c61-domperidone-role|The chapter contrasts its use outside the US, including Canada, with its lack of routine US availability.":["u209"],"c61-domperidone-safety|FDA identifies cardiac arrhythmia, cardiac arrest and sudden-death risks with domperidone.":["u210"],"c61-domperidone-safety|It is not approved or legally marketed for human use in the US; selected gastrointestinal expanded-access use is a separate route and does not establish routine availability.":["u211"],"c61-maob-benefit|Selegiline, rasagiline and safinamide provide modest symptomatic benefit through MAO-B inhibition and can be used with levodopa.":["u212"],"c61-maob-benefit|Selegiline and rasagiline reduce motor fluctuations.":["u213"],"c61-maob-benefit|Trials have not convincingly established neuroprotection; symptomatic benefit complicates its assessment.":["u214"],"c61-maob-metabolites|Selegiline, unlike rasagiline, is metabolised to L-amphetamine and methamphetamine.":["u215"],"c61-maob-metabolites|MAO-B selectivity distinguishes this group from the diet-linked hypertensive effects of MAO-A inhibition.":["u216"],"c61-maob-metabolites|The chapter proposes that safinamide’s greater selectivity may reduce off-target dietary effects; it does not establish an absence of interaction risk.":["u217"],"c61-amantadine-mechanisms|Amantadine has mild antimuscarinic effects, promotes dopamine release, inhibits dopamine uptake and blocks NMDA receptors.":["u218"],"c61-amantadine-mechanisms|The chapter reports symptomatic benefit in approximately two-thirds of patients and describes amantadine as its only established effective antidyskinetic agent, through antiglutamatergic action.":["u219"],"c61-amantadine-adverse|Livedo reticularis, ankle oedema, visual hallucinations and confusion can occur.":["u220"],"c61-amantadine-adverse|Older patients often tolerate amantadine poorly because of its cognitive and psychiatric effects; antidyskinetic benefit may diminish over time.":["u221"],"c61-amantadine-renal|Geriatric dose qualifier: the immediate-release label calls for reduced dosing with renal impairment and in people aged 65 years or older because amantadine accumulates as renal function declines.":["u222"],"c61-amantadine-renal|Heart failure, oedema and orthostatic hypotension may also require reduction.":["u223"],"c61-amantadine-renal|The chapter regimens below are not default doses for every older patient.":["u224"],"c61-wearing-off-threshold|The chapter’s printed definition of wearing-off is recurrence or worsening of bradykinesia, rigidity or tremor when an adequate levodopa dose does not last at least 6 hours.":["u225"],"c61-wearing-off-threshold|This is the chapter’s duration-based description, not a universal diagnostic cutoff.":["u226"],"c61-wearing-off-course|Early in treatment, a long-duration response makes exact dose timing less important.":["u227"],"c61-wearing-off-course|Over time this response is lost, the short-duration response dominates, and “off” episodes become more frequent or severe.":["u228"],"c61-nonmotor-off-recognition|Behavioural and sensory “offs” can occur without any motor off: parkinsonism need not return.":["u278"],"c61-nonmotor-off-recognition|They are easily missed because the treating physician sees no visible motor change.":["u279"],"c61-nonmotor-off-symptoms|Pain, akathisia, depression, anxiety, dysphoria and panic can occur, usually in combination.":["u280"],"c61-nonmotor-off-symptoms|Sensory offs, like dystonic offs, can be very disabling.":["u281"],"c61-dopamine-dysregulation-chain|These sensory and behavioural phenomena may drive repeated levodopa use more strongly than motor parkinsonian or dystonic offs.":["u282"],"c61-dopamine-dysregulation-chain|Unrecognised nonmotor off symptoms can therefore lead to escalating levodopa use; a few patients develop an addictive relationship with dopaminergic medication—dopamine dysregulation syndrome.":["u283"],"c61-chorea-description|Dyskinesias are involuntary movements with two major forms.":["u284"],"c61-chorea-description|Chorea consists of irregular, nonrhythmic, unsustained, dance-like movements that seem to flow from one body part to another and can resemble benign fidgeting.":["u285"],"c61-dystonia-description|Dystonia consists of more sustained, twisting contractions.":["u286"],"c61-dystonia-description|Many patients have both chorea and dystonia.":["u287"],"c61-peak-dose-dyskinesia|Peak-dose dyskinesia occurs when dopaminergic effect is excessive: reduce the individual levodopa dose, often using smaller, more frequent doses.":["u229"],"c61-peak-dose-dyskinesia|The chapter also describes adjunctive amantadine and reports that buspirone up to 20 mg/day may help selected patients; this is a possible benefit, not a routine treatment mandate.":["u230"],"c61-peak-dose-adjunct-regimen|For peak-dose dyskinesia, the chapter offers amantadine 100 mg twice daily, increased to 200 mg twice daily if necessary.":["u288"],"c61-peak-dose-adjunct-regimen|It also reports that buspirone up to 20 mg/day may help some patients.":["u289"],"c61-peak-dose-adjunct-regimen|These are chapter regimens, not universal geriatric doses: apply the renal and older-adult amantadine dose reductions described above.":["u290"],"c61-dystonia-botulinum|Depending on the distribution and disability, dystonic dyskinesia can be treated with local chemodenervation, such as botulinum toxin.":["u291"],"c61-dystonia-botulinum|Quality of life may improve substantially, but weakening the injected muscle group can impair function—particularly when treating dystonic movements of the hands.":["u292"],"c61-dopaminergic-mental-effects|Dopaminergic medication-related complications include psychosis, confusion, agitation, hallucinations, paranoid delusions, punding, impulse-control disorders and excessive sleeping.":["u293"],"c61-dopaminergic-mental-mechanism|These effects are probably related to dopamine-receptor activation in anteroventral striatal regions or nonstriatal regions, particularly cortical and limbic structures.":["u294"],"c61-drug-hallucination-character|Drug-induced hallucinations tend to be mild and visual rather than auditory.":["u295"],"c61-psychosis-sequence|First look for delirium and reversible triggers, then review medications that may worsen psychosis.":["u47"],"c61-psychosis-sequence|Persistent distressing psychosis may require a specialist-selected drug that minimizes worsening of parkinsonism.":["u48"],"c61-psychosis-medication-review|For PD psychosis, reduce total dopaminergic treatment as far as motor function allows, and review other contributors to encephalopathy, including benzodiazepines, anticholinergics and opioids.":["u231"],"c61-psychosis-drug-evidence|The chapter describes quetiapine starting at 25 mg at bedtime, or pimavanserin, a 5-HT2A inverse agonist and antagonist.":["u232"],"c61-psychosis-drug-evidence|It contrasts prospective trial support for pimavanserin with the weaker efficacy evidence for quetiapine.":["u233"],"c61-quetiapine-start|Adjunctive treatment for drug-induced hallucinations can begin with the addition of quetiapine, starting with 25 mg at bedtime.":["u49"],"c61-quetiapine-caution|The chapter’s starting-dose example is not a guarantee of benefit or safety.":["u50"],"c61-quetiapine-caution|Monitor sedation, orthostatic symptoms and falls; quetiapine is a selected option rather than a reason to use antipsychotics routinely.":["u51"],"c61-pimavanserin-historical|Historical chapter regimen: pimavanserin 17 mg one to two times daily, with reduction for CYP3A4 inhibitors.":["u234"],"c61-pimavanserin-historical|This has been superseded by the current US label below and should not be used as the current dosing schedule.":["u235"],"c61-pimavanserin-regimen|Current US label: pimavanserin 34 mg orally once daily, without titration, for hallucinations and delusions associated with PD psychosis.":["u236"],"c61-pimavanserin-regimen|With strong CYP3A4 inhibitors, reduce to 10 mg once daily; avoid strong or moderate CYP3A4 inducers.":["u237"],"c61-pimavanserin-regimen|QT prolongation and the antipsychotic mortality warning in older patients with dementia remain relevant.":["u238"],"c61-clozapine-monitoring|Clozapine needs blood-count monitoring, but the index’s indefinite weekly schedule is not reproduced as a universal rule.":["u53"],"c61-sleep-movement-frequency|Periodic leg movements of sleep and restless legs syndrome are estimated to be twice as common in Parkinson disease as in matched populations.":["u54"],"c61-rbd-caution|For dream enactment, prioritize a safe sleeping environment and consider the sleep chapter’s treatment discussion.":["u55"],"c61-rbd-caution|Sedating medication is a particular concern when falls, cognitive impairment or sleep-disordered breathing coexist.":["u56"],"c61-rbd-drugs|The chapter describes clonazepam 0.5–2 mg at bedtime as a main treatment for REM sleep behaviour disorder.":["u239"],"c61-rbd-drugs|Melatonin may be tried first or combined with clonazepam; cholinesterase inhibitors may help selected patients.":["u240"],"c61-rbd-drugs|Dopaminergic therapy probably has little or no effect on RBD.":["u241"],"c61-rbd-safety|Protect the patient and bed partner: separate beds if needed, a mattress on the floor, first-floor sleeping and removal of dangerous objects.":["u242"],"c61-rbd-safety|Review antidepressants that may precipitate or worsen dream enactment.":["u243"],"c61-depression-options|The chapter identifies bupropion and tricyclic antidepressants as useful for PD depression.":["u246"],"c61-depression-options|When insomnia coexists, a sedating antidepressant may address both problems: nortriptyline has fewer anticholinergic effects than amitriptyline, and low-dose mirtazapine is another example.":["u247"],"c61-depression-anticholinergic-context|“Fewer anticholinergic effects” is a relative comparison, not an absence of anticholinergic burden.":["u248"],"c61-depression-anticholinergic-context|Weigh that burden in an older patient with cognitive symptoms, falls or freezing; the depression discussion does not cancel the antimuscarinic caution above.":["u249"],"c61-depression-cbt|The chapter reports emerging evidence that CBT may improve PD depression more than existing medication-based approaches.":["u250"],"c61-depression-cbt|This is a qualified comparative finding, not a guarantee that CBT outperforms medication for every patient.":["u251"],"c61-dbs-approval2002|The chapter’s 2002 FDA milestone refers to bilateral STN or GPi stimulation for advanced, levodopa-responsive Parkinson disease inadequately controlled with medication.":["u296"],"c61-dbs-approval2002|The FDA decision was on 14 January 2002.":["u297"],"c61-dbs-indications|Good surgical candidates can gain substantially in quality of life.":["u298"],"c61-dbs-indications|Typical indications are difficult-to-manage motor fluctuations and/or dyskinesias, or medication-refractory parkinsonian tremor.":["u299"],"c61-dbs-hardware|An implantable pulse generator (IPG), resembling a pacemaker, is placed in the chest.":["u300"],"c61-dbs-hardware|A lead is tunnelled under the skin to the subthalamic nucleus (STN) or internal globus pallidus (GPi).":["u301"],"c61-dbs-benefit|Optimised stimulation improves on function and reduces disabling off features.":["u302"],"c61-dbs-benefit|Patients may be able to lower dopaminergic medication doses and thereby reduce dyskinesia; the benefit and medication reduction are individual, not guaranteed.":["u303"],"c61-dbs-response-exception|Tremor is the exception: motor features that generally fail to improve with dopaminergic medication—postural instability, other axial motor features and some gait freezing—also generally fail to improve with DBS.":["u304"],"c61-dbs-nonmotor-limit|DBS typically has little effect on nonmotor features unless they are directly related to medication-associated on–off fluctuations.":["u305"],"c61-dbs-open-loop-historical|SUPERSEDED TIME FRAMING — the chapter describes DBS as currently open loop and closed-loop stimulation as the next advance.":["u306"],"c61-dbs-open-loop-historical|In conventional open-loop DBS, programmed stimulation type and intensity are delivered continuously once enabled.":["u307"],"c61-dbs-open-loop-historical|The definition remains useful; the claim that closed-loop treatment is only a future development does not.":["u308"],"c61-dbs-closed-loop-concept|The chapter’s closed-loop concept detects local neurophysiological biomarkers that change with neural activity and relevant voluntary movements, then adjusts stimulation in real time rather than delivering an unchanged programme.":["u309"],"c61-dbs-adaptive-update|FDA UPDATE — on 20 February 2025, the FDA approved an optional adaptive DBS feature for the indicated levodopa-responsive PD population.":["u310"],"c61-dbs-adaptive-update|It senses local field potentials and adjusts stimulation amplitude within clinician-set limits.":["u311"],"c61-dbs-adaptive-update|Open-loop DBS remains an option; adaptive DBS is no longer solely a future proposal.":["u312"],"c61-dbs-candidacy|Patients with significant speech, gait, depression with suicidal ideation, or cognitive difficulties are usually not good candidates for deep brain stimulation.":["u57"],"c61-dbs-poor-candidate-harm|Significant speech or gait difficulty, depression with suicidal ideation, or cognitive difficulty usually makes a patient a poor DBS candidate.":["u313"],"c61-dbs-poor-candidate-harm|The concern is not only nonresponse: these problems may become notably worse after DBS in some patients, including suicide risk.":["u314"],"c61-dbs-expert-selection|Selection is best guided by an experienced movement-disorder neurologist.":["u315"],"c61-atypical-dbs|Deep brain stimulation is not suggested for patients with atypical parkinsonian conditions since the majority of these motor features do not respond to dopaminergic medications.":["u58"],"c61-dbs-atypical-reason|DBS is not suggested for atypical parkinsonian conditions because most of their motor features do not respond to dopaminergic medication.":["u316"],"c61-fus-historical-correction|CORRECTED SOURCE DESCRIPTION — the supplied chapter review couples the 2018 FUS approval to “radiofrequency ablation to the STN” and says it involves no anaesthesia.":["u317"],"c61-fus-historical-correction|Do not learn that combined description: the independent FDA and manufacturer sources below distinguish ultrasound energy, the approved target and anaesthesia.":["u318"],"c61-fus-fda-scope|FDA approval on 16 December 2018 covered unilateral VIM thalamotomy for medication-refractory tremor in tremor-dominant Parkinson disease, in adults aged at least 30.":["u319"],"c61-fus-fda-scope|This was a target-specific approval; it was not an approval of STN ablation or every possible FUS target.":["u320"],"c61-fus-mechanism|MRI-guided focused ultrasound concentrates ultrasound energy to create a thermal lesion.":["u321"],"c61-fus-mechanism|It is ultrasound ablation, not radiofrequency ablation; target selection and approval scope must be checked for the particular procedure.":["u322"],"c61-fus-anaesthesia|The procedure is performed awake without a requirement for general anaesthesia.":["u323"],"c61-fus-anaesthesia|Local anaesthetic is used to attach the stereotactic frame, so “no anaesthesia” is too absolute.":["u324"],"c61-fus-selection|FUS may suit appropriately selected patients who cannot or do not wish to undergo conventional DBS.":["u325"],"c61-fus-selection|Its lack of an implanted generator avoids later IPG replacement and repeated stimulation-programming visits.":["u326"]}}
